WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.570
. Let's go next to Preston from Birmingham.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:12.150
Now, is this Birmingham, Alabama, or United
Kingdom?

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.540
Preston from Birmingham.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.330
Hi, can you hear me, David?

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.330
Yes, I can.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.570
It sounds like it's Alabama, not not UK.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.150
Yeah, you can tell no accent.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.490
That was I was immediately able to deduce
that.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.490
All right, David.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.840
Well, I took the time to formalize my thoughts
for this question.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.940
Oh, thank you.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.660
I went ahead and wrote a thesis for you.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.340
They'll submit for you to review.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.539
Thank you.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:39.930
So I have a few questions regarding Bernie
Sanders raised the Wage Act.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:40.930
Sure.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:45.960
So the Raise the Wage Act would add to twenty
five to the federal minimum wage every year

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.269
through twenty twenty five, right after that
the wage would be increased by the annual

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:57.489
percentage increase of the median hourly wage
for all employees.

00:00:57.489 --> 00:00:58.489
Yes.

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:06.180
So my first question about this is, why are
they deciding to increase the wage by the

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:12.830
annual percentage increase of the median hourly
wage as opposed to something like adjusting

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:13.900
for inflation?

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:22.159
And also, why are they specifically excluding
annual percentage increases in salaries from

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:23.259
that calculation?

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:27.200
Well, I think that the there's a bunch of
different stuff there.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.729
First of all, when we talk about the hourly
wage, you're talking about the wage for people

00:01:34.729 --> 00:01:36.189
paid hourly.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:43.259
So it is logical to me that you wouldn't index
it to salaried folks who might work 35 or

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:44.810
60 hours per week.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:45.810
That part makes sense.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.640
So maybe we can take that piece off the table.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.210
I think you would look at if you're going
to index an hourly wage, you would look at

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.210
hourly workers.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:53.210
Is that fair?

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.210
Yes.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.299
OK, in terms of doing it to median rather
than inflation, I don't know the answer.

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:00.740
I've not I've not read about the reason.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.930
The reason to do it median rather than mean
seems obvious.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:10.050
And as people have given this example before,
if you put one hundred people in a room and

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:14.080
one of them is Bill Gates, the median wage
will be really high.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.580
But the median may not be because so so median
rather than mean makes sense.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.490
But as far as versus inflation, I've seen
a lot of proposals that would index the minimum

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.490
wage to inflation.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:28.090
I don't know why it's being done to the median
hourly wage, but I would be interested in

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.390
learning why I would.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.820
To completely agree with everything you're
saying, though.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.820
Makes sense.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.300
I've got one more question for you about this.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.500
So Sanders is proposing that to twenty five
increase every year through twenty twenty

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.700
five.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.010
What's your opinion on this time frame of
the roll out?

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:55.060
Do you think it would be faster to or it would
be better to increase this wage faster, maybe

00:02:55.060 --> 00:03:00.240
as a way to benefit low wage earners and allow
them to put money back into the local economy

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.900
during the pandemic?

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.760
And on the other hand, I understand that small
businesses would be hurt by sudden wage increases,

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.330
as was addressed in the town hall last night.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.330
Yeah.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:11.630
So what's your opinion on this time frame?

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.290
Do you think this is a realistic goal?

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.450
Do you think it should go faster or slower?

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:20.340
So I would love for it to go faster, but I
recognize that it's extraordinarily unlikely

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.480
to be politically viable.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:26.260
And when you look at senators like Kirsten
Sinema and Joe Manchin, who aren't even sure

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:32.090
they they want a 15 dollar minimum wage, the
idea of making it a 15 dollar federal minimum

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.680
wage tomorrow just seems so unlikely.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.830
So I'm not surprised that they're looking
at phasing it in over time.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:44.900
Now, of course, by twenty twenty five, a fifteen
dollar minimum wage is going to be a lot less

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.720
than it would be today because of inflation,
not a lot less.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:52.280
I mean, the US has relatively low inflation,
but it's already going to be time to look

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.380
at the next step at that point in time.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:01.810
But I'm not surprised because particularly
in places where the state minimum, a lot of

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:07.570
states already have minimum wages of 10, 11,
12, 14, even in places where they the the

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:11.220
state minimum wage really is still in the
sevens.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:16.039
You're talking about more than doubling it
and whatever you believe the minimum wage

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:19.750
ultimately should be, and I believe it should
be much higher.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.530
Doubling the minimum wage will have an economic
impact.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.760
And so I'm not surprised at all that it would
be phased in over time.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.390
And if Bernie is behind it, I think that he
started out well.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:30.850
Yeah, I agree.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.250
David, thank you for the time, taking time
to answer my questions.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:35.760
So if you have a good day, you too.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:36.760
All right.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:37.760
Very good.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.240
Great to hear from from our friends in Alabama,
as always.

